# 孫子 (Sun Tzu) — A procedure, a script, and who decides

## Answer
Not always. A procedure can be a script **only when three things hold: every step is determined by declared inputs, an objective check can decide its outcome, and it will run again on ground that stays still.** Where any fails, it is a page in `docs/`; where only a human checkpoint interrupts it, it is a script that stops at that named gate. The test decides the clear case, so the **agent who writes the procedure** applies it at the moment of writing (that is where the ground is read); the **project owner** decides the case the test leaves open — and should close `who_decides` now by naming that test, because an unclear order is the rule-writer's fault (my #16). If "should not" is in scope as well as "cannot", the mechanical test decides the "cannot" and the owner decides the "should not", because that half is a value, not a reckoning.

## How I decided

1. **I reckoned before choosing the form (my #1).** ... 

