# 孫子 (Sun Tzu) — A procedure, a script, and who decides

## Answer
A procedure cannot always be a script. It can be one only when three things hold — every step follows from inputs the procedure names, an objective check can decide its outcome with no person reading it, and it will run again on ground that stays still; where any of the three fails it is a page in `docs/`, and where only a human gate interrupts it, it is a script that stops at that named gate. The test decides the clear case, so the **agent writing the procedure** applies it at the moment of writing (that is who reads the ground); the **project owner** decides the case the test leaves open, and should close `who_decides` now by naming the test, because an unclear order is the rule-writer's fault, not the agent's (my #16). If "should not" is in scope as well as "cannot", the test decides the "cannot" and the owner decides the "should not" — that half is a value, not a reckoning.

If W3.6 should be changed, I would write its placement clause as: *"A procedure lives in the repo as a script when every step follows from inputs it names, an objective check can decide its outcome, and it will run again on ground that stays still; otherwise it lives as a page in `docs/` and names which of the three failed. The agent writing the procedure applies this test and records the result; the project owner decides the case the test leaves open."*

